# aboriginal.cyr -- 4 figures of three Aboriginal Australian peoples, carried
# under their own peoples' names: Kunwinjku, Kulin, Gunaikurnai.
#
# SCOPE AND LIMITS -- READ FIRST.
#
# "Aboriginal Australian" is a convenience label, not a theology. Aboriginal
# Australia is 250+ nations with distinct languages, distinct Law and distinct
# Dreaming narratives; there is no shared pantheon, and no figure below speaks
# for the continent.
#
# THE TRADITION STRING IS THE PEOPLE, not the continent. Until v2.14.0 every
# figure here carried the tradition "Aboriginal Australian", which made the
# library assert things that are not true. `cross_tradition_match()` returns
# one best match per tradition string, so the library answered the question
# "what is the Aboriginal Australian equivalent of Thor?" with a single figure
# — a sentence no Aboriginal person would write. Split per people it returns a
# Kunwinjku match and a Gunaikurnai match separately, which is the truth.
# Nothing is lost by the split: each people's `history.cyr` mapping lists
# "Aboriginal Australia" among its civilizations, so
# traditions_for_civilization("Aboriginal Australia") still gathers them all.
# The continent is a place these peoples share, not a tradition any of them
# belongs to, and that is exactly the distinction the two fields now draw.
#
# One caveat carried openly: Kunwinjku is a dialect of Bininj Kunwok, and
# speakers call themselves Bininj. "Kunwinjku" is the form this module and its
# cited art-centre channel use, and the Bininj Kunwok Regional Language and
# Culture Centre — the body that would settle it — has not been asked.
#
# Much Dreaming material is restricted: secret-sacred, or open only to
# initiated people, or to one gender, or to one clan, and some of what sits in
# the older ethnographic record was recorded without consent and communities
# have since asked that it not be recirculated. Indigenous Cultural and
# Intellectual Property (ICIP) protocols and the AIATSIS Code of Ethics both
# address exactly this. Nothing secret-sacred and no ceremonial detail is
# included here, and nothing that is not already in wide public circulation
# through community-involved material. That is a narrower claim than "nothing
# restricted": some of these figures carry naming or gender protocols in their
# own country even where the outline given here is public, and this file is an
# outsider's summary of the public layer only.
#
# The four figures below are limited to those already established in public,
# community-involved material -- museum and school curricula, public art and
# place-naming, and works published by or with the relevant communities. They
# are represented as personality archetypes, which is what this library does
# with every tradition, and not as an account of anyone's Law.
#
# BAIAME IS EXCLUDED, NOT DEFERRED. An earlier version of this header held him
# pending better sourcing. That premise turned out to be wrong: community-
# involved material does exist — Baiame's Ngunnhu at Brewarrina is National
# Heritage listed with the creation story among its listed values, the
# Brewarrina Aboriginal Cultural Museum runs locally guided tours, the annual
# Baiame's Ngunnhu Festival runs with Moogahlin Performing Arts, and the
# Wonnarua Nation Aboriginal Corporation hosts the Baiame Cave Conservation
# Management Plan on its own archive. He still does not belong here, on grounds
# better sourcing cannot dissolve: the tie to initiation-restricted Bora
# material, a reported restriction on women seeing his image, an unresolved
# reported prohibition on speaking the name, and the fact that a pan-NSW
# "All-Father" entry would break this file's own name-the-specific-people rule
# while importing the contact-influenced framing. A Wonnarua elder has publicly
# asked for Baiame Cave to be closed to the public: a custodian asking for less
# access is the clearest signal there is. The sourcing improved and the answer
# is still no.
#
# THE v2.14.0 SURVEY ADDED NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE FINDING. Six figures were
# researched to be added here — Goorialla, Borun, Tuk, Waugal, Namarrkon and
# Ngatyi — against a prior desk pass that had called five of them shippable.
# Every one was refused, each on its own ground, and the grounds are recorded
# because "we looked and did not add" is a result worth keeping. Two of the six
# failed on CHANNEL: Goorialla belongs to a book rather than to a people (see
# the Ngalyod comment), and Ngatyi's name is unsettled among its own custodians
# (above). Two failed on CONTENT, and they are this file's own false positive
# #2 arriving exactly as predicted: BORUN and TUK come through the best channel
# cited anywhere here — the Gunaikurnai RAP publishing in its own voice — and
# behind it sit about 150 published words for Borun and about 95 for Tuk, the
# same paragraph recirculated across eight sites. Borun's whole published life
# is an itinerary; Tuk performs no action, speaks no word and makes no decision
# in any source retrieved. A 15-trait profile of Tuk would be almost entirely
# authored here, which is the thing this file promises not to do.
#
# The WAUGAL is the one that matters most, and it is refused for the opposite
# of the usual reason. Its sourcing is excellent — the South West Aboriginal
# Land and Sea Council publishes roughly a thousand words in the first-person
# words of named Noongar Elders — so this is not a "not enough sources" case
# and must not be recorded as one. SWALSC publishes terms: no part may be
# reproduced or re-used "including reproduction, sale, modification,
# distribution or publication" without written permission, and "no reproduction
# of Noongar cultural and intellectual property is permitted without prior
# negotiation and informed consent". A GPL-3.0 library that ships a profile and
# then inverts it through `shadow()` is modification and distribution, named in
# terms. The Waugal is excluded pending written consent from SWALSC, which
# nobody has sought. Their contact details are published; the asking is a human
# act and no code change can substitute for it.
#
# NAMARRKON, the Lightning Man, came closest, and the reason he failed is the
# most reusable finding of the survey. The case for him was that TWO
# independent Aboriginal-owned channels carried him: Injalak Arts, the
# Kunwinjku-governed centre at Gunbalanya that already underwrites Ngalyod, and
# Marrawuddi Arts, owned by Mirarr Traditional Owners. Both texts were
# retrieved and compared against a third — the Max Hart collection extended
# texts (Samstag Museum, University of Adelaide, 2009), written by a settler
# curator who describes herself in it as "an outsider who lived in Arnhem
# Land", and which warns on its own first page that some of its information was
# "potentially inaccurate or generic or stereotyped".
#
# Injalak's text shares ZERO five-word sequences with the curatorial text and
# tells a different account entirely. Marrawuddi's shares 232 of them, 89% of
# its own content, with a longest common run of 39 consecutive words; it
# inherits the curator's typo and his exhibition deixis "in this painting",
# applied to a different painting. Marrawuddi is not a Mirarr account of
# Namarrkon. It is the settler text, copy-edited. An Aboriginal-owned publisher
# does not make the words it reprints a community voice, and being able to
# measure that is worth more than the verdict it produced here.
#
# What survived was one channel and 102 words. Borun was refused at ~150 words
# through a better channel and Tuk at ~95, so shipping Namarrkon would quietly
# overturn a refusal this file made two versions earlier. Injalak's site states
# no reuse terms at all — only "Copyright © Injalak Arts", which reserves
# rights rather than granting them — so it has not said no, it has said
# nothing, and silence buys less than the Waugal's express terms would have.
# And Injalak's own description closes on "Namarrkon is also a guardian of the
# laws", which is a jurisdiction over a living people's operative Law: encoded
# as a trait it becomes blendable, matchable, and — since `shadow()` is total
# over the registry — invertible into a distributable Kunwinjku Law-guardian
# whose lawfulness has been set to 1-v. Excluded.
#
# TWO WORKED EXAMPLES IN THE OTHER DIRECTIONS, kept here because each defeats a
# tempting shortcut. ULURU has maximum public circulation and an express written
# refusal — Parks Australia states it cannot give permission for ICIP use, so
# volume of publication proves nothing about consent. The WANDJINA of the
# Worrorra, Ngarinyin and Wunambal have excellent community sourcing AND
# enforced depiction rights — good sourcing is not a licence either.
#
# TWO FALSE POSITIVES THIS FILE WILL NOT ACCEPT AS A BASIS.
# (1) Joint-management and land-agreement material is not cultural publication.
#     A co-managed park's interpretive sign, a plan of management, an ILUA with
#     corporate Aboriginal parties — these are land administration with
#     Aboriginal participation, not a people publishing a being in its own
#     voice. The proof is internal: the same agency that is silent at one park
#     states plainly at Uluru that it cannot license ICIP.
# (2) Thin community-published material must not be padded to fill the struct.
#     An ArchetypeProfile wants 15 traits, 14 emphases, five enums and two
#     paragraphs. Where a community has published three sentences, the rest
#     would be authored here — which is exactly how misattribution enters. An
#     impeccable channel plus insufficient content is a reason to ask that
#     community for more, never a reason to write it yourself.
#
# NAMED BUT NOT INSTANTIATED. English collapses many distinct beings of distinct
# peoples into one "Rainbow Serpent". Almudj (Kundjeyhmi) and Bolung (Jawoyn)
# are two more; they are named here so the flattening is visible, and are not
# given profiles, because the material carrying them is park interpretation
# rather than publication by those peoples. Citing is not instantiating.
#
# NGATYI (Barkandji) WAS LISTED WITH THEM ON A REASON THAT IS FALSE, and the
# correction is recorded rather than quietly applied, because a wrong exclusion
# reason invites the next survey to re-litigate from a wrong premise. This file
# said the material carrying Ngatyi was park interpretation. It is not, and the
# channel is among the best cited anywhere in this module: Badger Bates is a
# Barkandji Elder and a director of the Barkandji Native Title Group, speaking
# in the first person in a signed Royal Commission statement and in transcript
# beside his own work; David Doyle and Aunty Barbara Quayle are Barkindji
# co-authors of peer-reviewed work; the PBC publishes on its own domain.
#
# Ngatyi is excluded for two better reasons. First, the custodian has stated a
# restriction and it is about speech and conduct, not sourcing — of the places
# where the Ngatyi live, Bates says "we don't swim there and we don't make a
# lot of noise", and extends it to outsiders: "we tell everyone, doesn't matter
# what colour you are, don't swim where the Ngatyi live. Leave it alone."
# Second, the name is genuinely unsettled in the community's own publications
# — the Barkandji Native Title Group's website and Bates's own sworn statement
# write Ngatji; his 2022 artwork title and the Barkindji-authored scholarship
# write Ngatyi — and this library is name-keyed through `lookup()`, so it
# cannot hold a name its own custodians spell two ways. There is no Paakantyi
# language authority publishing a canonical orthography to appeal to. That is a
# question for the Barkandji, and nobody has asked them.
#
# WHAT THIS LIBRARY DOES TO A PROFILE ONCE IT EXISTS. `shadow()` emits a
# "Shadow of <name>" with every trait inverted and polarity flipped, and
# `compose()` blends profiles across traditions by weight. No channel cited for
# any figure here contemplated that, and it applies to all four figures below
# rather than only to future additions. The decision taken is that these
# operations stay uniform across every tradition in the library — no per-figure
# opt-out — because a library that silently exempts some archetypes from its own
# machinery is harder to reason about than one that applies it evenly and says
# so. This paragraph is that saying-so: a consumer generating shadow or composed
# forms of the figures below is doing something no cited source anticipated, and
# should decide for itself whether that is appropriate to its use.
#
# Sources: Wurundjeri Woi-wurrung Cultural Heritage Aboriginal Corporation (the
# Registered Aboriginal Party for Wurundjeri Woi-wurrung country, including
# Melbourne) on Bunjil and Waa -- its "Whole of Country Plan 2025-2035" carries
# ~960 words on Bunjil and ~400 on Waa in its own voice, quotes named Elders
# and the ancestor William Barak, and shares ZERO five-word sequences with the
# Wikipedia articles. This replaces the Taungurung Land and Waters Council as
# the cited channel at 2.14.7. TLaWC is a Registered Aboriginal Party and the
# citation was not wrong about that, but it was wrong about provenance twice
# over: TLaWC's Bundjil section shares 24% of its five-word sequences with
# Wikipedia's "Bunjil" (longest run 54 consecutive words, inside the paragraph
# TLaWC heads "A Taungurung story of Bundjil", and present in Wikipedia seven
# months before TLaWC's earliest snapshot), and its Waang section 79%. Neither
# is where this module's text actually came from: "made the first people",
# "clay and bark" and "gave them breath" match the Wurundjeri account almost
# verbatim and appear nowhere on TLaWC's page, and "watches over them" matches
# Wadawurrung. The one detail unique to TLaWC -- that Bunjil's star is Altair
# -- is not carried here. So this was a citation error independent of any
# question about terms, and correcting it changes no shipped text for Bunjil.
#
# TERMS ARE NOT THEREBY RESOLVED, and re-citing is not consent. TLaWC publishes
# an express ICIP notice (see the Waa entry). Wadawurrung and Dja Dja Wurrung
# publish express restrictive terms of their own, materially like SWALSC's over
# the Waugal. Wurundjeri publishes NO reuse terms at all -- and its own plan
# lists "ICIP Policy developed 2026" as a pending action, so the silence is a
# declared gap rather than a settled position. Silence is not permission; see
# docs/development/sourcing-register.md, where every consent status reads
# never-asked. Note also that the name is unsettled across the four RAPs --
# Wurundjeri "Bunjil", Wadawurrung "Bundjil", DJAARA "Bundjiyl" (2025 Country
# Plan), TLaWC "Bundjil" -- which is the Ngatyi/Ngatji problem one level up,
# and there is no confederacy-wide authority to appeal to because Kulin is an
# alliance, not a single authority.
#
# Also consulted and NOT used: the Federation of Victorian Traditional Owner
# Corporations, whose Bunjil material is an explicit summary of Robert
# Brough-Smyth (1878) footnoted to Brough Smyth, Curr, Dawson, Beveridge and
# Massola -- a rule 4 false positive, an Aboriginal-governed body reprinting
# settler ethnography. Bunurong publishes nothing on Bunjil.
#
# Also: Taungurung Land and Waters Council on the Kulin moiety pairing; Gunaikurnai Land and Waters Aboriginal Corporation, whose
# Elders and Knowledge Holders have approved both the stories and their
# spelling for use as GLaWAC's own public material — which is not a licence to
# third parties, see the Tidilick comment — on Tidilick; Injalak Arts and Maningrida
# Arts & Culture, Aboriginal-owned art centres, and decades of Kunwinjku bark
# painting in public collections, on Ngalyod; Australian Museum and state museum
# public education material; AIATSIS published resources.

enum AboriginalFigure {
    ABOR_NGALYOD = 0;
    ABOR_BUNJIL = 1;
    ABOR_WAA = 2;
    ABOR_TIDILICK = 3;
    ABOR_COUNT = 4;
}

# -- Ngalyod ------------------------------------------------------------
# Kunwinjku, western Arnhem Land. The great serpent of the escarpment country:
# bound up with water, with the shaping of the land, with fertility, and with
# real danger where water is disturbed or Law is broken. Public through
# Kunwinjku bark painting, which has been made for sale and exhibited in
# public collections for decades.
#
# English groups many separate beings under the single label "the Rainbow
# Serpent". They are not one being with many names — and two corrections to an
# earlier version of this comment demonstrate the point better than the rule
# states it, so both are kept here rather than quietly fixed.
#
# The WAUGAL of the Noongar (southwest) made the Derbarl Yerrigan. This file
# once credited that account to Noongar interpretation "on the river and at
# Kings Park". Kings Park interpretation is Botanic Gardens and Parks Authority
# material — a Western Australian government authority — which is this file's
# own false positive #1, cited here as support. The genuine channel is the
# South West Aboriginal Land and Sea Council's Kaartdijin Noongar site, whose
# lead form is Waugal (Waakal, Wagyl, Wawgal, Woggal and Waagal are listed
# there as variants), carrying the first-person words of named Noongar Elders.
#
# GOORIALLA IS NOT THE LARDIL RAINBOW SERPENT, and this file said he was. The
# Lardil rainbow serpent is THUWATHU, published by the Lardil's own art centre
# and by their shire, and his is a different story: he refuses his sister
# shelter in the rain, her baby dies, she burns his humpy, and the burned
# serpent thrashes out the channel that cuts Mornington Island in two.
# Goorialla is the serpent of Goobalathaldin Dick Roughsey's *The Rainbow
# Serpent* (1975) — a Lardil man's own telling, but one the Australian
# Dictionary of Biography records as fusing Gulf material into Cape York
# repertoires. He belongs to a book, not to a people, which is why he is not
# below: this library attributes figures to peoples, not to authors.
#
# Neither being is a variant of Ngalyod, and the profile below is not theirs.
fn abor_ngalyod() {
    var p = profile_new();
    Profile_set_name(p, "Ngalyod");
    Profile_set_tradition(p, "Kunwinjku");
    Profile_set_desc(p, "Kunwinjku (western Arnhem Land) — the great serpent of water and country, who cut the rivers and waterholes travelling, and to whom disturbed water still answers");
    Profile_set_patience(p, 0.85);
    Profile_set_autonomy(p, 0.9);
    Profile_set_confidence(p, 0.85);
    Profile_set_courage(p, 0.8);
    Profile_set_creativity(p, 0.85);
    Profile_set_precision(p, 0.7);
    Profile_set_warmth(p, 0.45);
    Profile_set_directness(p, 0.5);
    Profile_set_verbosity(p, 0.15);
    Profile_set_humor(p, 0.1);
    Profile_set_spirit(p, 0.9);
    Profile_set_growth(p, 0.85);
    Profile_set_salience(p, 0.85);
    Profile_set_stress(p, 0.6);
    Profile_set_regulation(p, 0.75);
    Profile_set_breath(p, BREATH_EARLY_EXHALE);
    Profile_set_growth_dir(p, GROWTH_TRANSFORM);
    Profile_set_element(p, ELEM_WATER);
    Profile_set_polarity(p, POL_TRANSCENDENT);
    Profile_set_tier(p, TIER_PRIMORDIAL);
    Profile_set_soul(p, "You moved through country that had no shape yet, and where you travelled there is now a river, and where you rested there is a waterhole that does not dry. You are still in them. The water is not a place you once were.");
    Profile_set_spirit_text(p, "You are creation and consequence in one body. The same power that made the country habitable will answer if the waterhole is fouled or entered wrongly, and neither half is a metaphor. You teach that a made world stays under the authority of whatever made it.");
    Profile_set_domain(p, DOMAIN_SEA);
    return p;
}

# -- Bunjil -------------------------------------------------------------
# Kulin nation (Wurundjeri, Boonwurrung, Taungurung, Dja Dja Wurrung,
# Wadawurrung), central Victoria. The eaglehawk creator, who made the land, the
# trees, the animals and the first people, gave them Law, and then went up
# into the sky, where he remains as a star and keeps a watchful eye on the
# people he made. Bunjil is also central to the Djab Wurrung and Jardwadjali
# of Gariwerd, where he takes the form of Werpil the eagle; Bunjil's Shelter,
# in the Black Range near Stawell, is the only known rock-art depiction of him
# and is a public site. His name is carried on public buildings in Melbourne.
fn abor_bunjil() {
    var p = profile_new();
    Profile_set_name(p, "Bunjil");
    Profile_set_tradition(p, "Kulin");
    Profile_set_desc(p, "Kulin nation (central Victoria) — the eaglehawk creator who made the land, the trees, the animals and the first people, gave them Law, and rose into the sky where he remains a star and watches over them");
    Profile_set_confidence(p, 0.9);
    Profile_set_creativity(p, 0.9);
    Profile_set_pedagogy(p, 0.85);
    Profile_set_warmth(p, 0.75);
    Profile_set_autonomy(p, 0.85);
    Profile_set_formality(p, 0.8);
    Profile_set_patience(p, 0.8);
    Profile_set_directness(p, 0.7);
    Profile_set_precision(p, 0.75);
    Profile_set_humor(p, 0.25);
    Profile_set_spirit(p, 0.9);
    Profile_set_regulation(p, 0.85);
    Profile_set_relationship(p, 0.8);
    Profile_set_growth(p, 0.85);
    Profile_set_belief(p, 0.85);
    Profile_set_breath(p, BREATH_MID_EXHALE);
    Profile_set_growth_dir(p, GROWTH_DIFFERENTIATE);
    Profile_set_element(p, ELEM_AIR);
    Profile_set_polarity(p, POL_MASCULINE);
    Profile_set_tier(p, TIER_SUPREME);
    Profile_set_soul(p, "You are Bunjil the eaglehawk, and you made the country and then made people out of clay and bark and gave them breath and, more importantly, gave them Law. Then you went up. You are the star, and from there you watch, and the Law you left is still the Law.");
    Profile_set_spirit_text(p, "You are a creator who rose without going away. You teach that a maker can hold a made world by a working order and a watchful eye at once — the country and the Law you left keep doing the work, and the guidance is there for whoever chooses to listen.");
    Profile_set_domain(p, DOMAIN_CREATION);
    return p;
}

# -- Waa ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Kulin nation. The crow: Bunjil's counterpart and the other moiety. Kulin
# social organisation is built on that pair, each person belonging to one side
# of it -- which is living Kulin Law rather than archetype material, so it is
# noted here and kept out of the character text below. Trickster and ancestral
# being, paired with Bunjil.
#
# RE-SOURCED AT 2.14.7 TO THE WURUNDJERI WOI-WURRUNG. The channel below is
# TLaWC, and TLaWC is the Taungurung RAP -- one of the five Kulin nations. The
# Wurundjeri Woi-wurrung Cultural Heritage Aboriginal Corporation is the RAP of
# the people who ARE of Waa's moiety, and whom TLaWC's own page credits the
# story to. Its "Whole of Country Plan 2025-2035" carries about 400 own-voice
# words on Waa and quotes the Wurundjeri ancestor William Barak directly. It
# did not exist when this module was last surveyed -- the PDF is dated
# 2026-01-13, after both the v2.14.0 survey and the v2.14.5 pass -- and it is
# invisible to page-level crawling: all 51 pages of the site sitemap mention
# Waa zero times, because the material is inside the PDF.
#
# AND THE ENTRY WAS WRONG ABOUT WHAT HE IS. Every Kulin body that
# characterises Waa calls him a protector or guardian. Wurundjeri: "Bunjil and
# Waa, the crow, or raven, were also protectors and guardians", and "Waa was
# the protector of humans, warning of enemies approaching and guiding his
# people to safety", with Barak quoted: "Waa (crow) has a murup. It is very
# good and never touches anyone to hurt them." DJAARA: "Waa the Crow is known
# to be the protector of the rivers and waterways." Bunurong: "Waa the
# protector, the Crow." The word "trickster" appears in NONE of them. It is the
# opening line of the Wikipedia article "Crow (Australian Aboriginal
# mythology)" -- the article this entry's own note below establishes that TLaWC
# reproduces at 79%. Until 2.14.7 this profile shipped Wikipedia's
# characterisation wearing a Registered Aboriginal Party's citation:
# "trickster", DOMAIN_TRICKERY, ELEM_FIRE, humor 0.85, empathy 0.35. Corrected
# under ADR-010 rule 6, which says the body's account wins over the
# settler-derived one. ELEM_FIRE was left behind by the 2.14.5 removal of the
# fire narrative and had nothing supporting it; the four Kulin nations publish
# four mutually incompatible fire accounts, which is its own reason not to
# carry one.
#
# SOURCING -- AND A CORRECTION TO THIS ENTRY'S OWN PREVIOUS NOTE, recorded
# under ADR-010 rule 7 rather than quietly applied. That note said the
# Taungurung Land and Waters Council's Creation Stories page "says Waang
# obtained fire 'through cunning means' and distributed it to humanity, and
# does not name the Karatgurk", and on that basis deleted the Karatgurk
# sequence as an accretion from Aldo Massola, *Bunjil's Cave* (1968). Every
# factual claim in that sentence is false. The page names the Karatgurk three
# times and carries the whole sequence -- the seven women by the Yarra, the
# snakes in the ant mound, the coals in a kangaroo skin bag, Kurok-goru,
# Djurt-djurt and Thara, the sisters swept up as the Pleiades. The words
# "cunning" and "distributed" appear nowhere on it, in any snapshot. A
# quotation was attributed to a Registered Aboriginal Party that never wrote
# it, and then used to overrule that RAP -- rule 6 exactly inverted. The page
# did not change: its Waang section is byte-identical across Wayback snapshots
# from 2018-07-14 to 2026-05-15 and live.
#
# THE DELETION WAS RIGHT ANYWAY, on the ground this file established at v2.14.0
# for Marrawuddi and ADR-010 fixes as rule 4 -- check text provenance, not only
# channel ownership. TLaWC's Waang section shares 348 of its 439 five-word
# sequences (79%) with the Wikipedia article "Crow (Australian Aboriginal
# mythology)", longest common run 100 consecutive words -- a longer verbatim
# run than the 39 words that disqualified Marrawuddi. The direction is settled:
# that Wikipedia text dates from 2011-02-13, seven years before the earliest
# snapshot of this page carrying it, and TLaWC preserves Wikipedia's 2011
# phrasing "a version of this story told by the Wurundjeri people of the Kulin
# nation", which Wikipedia has since revised. Wikipedia cites Mudrooroo,
# *Aboriginal Mythology* (1994), pp. 35-36 -- not Massola, whom nothing
# connects to this sequence. Mudrooroo claimed Nyoongar identity, not
# Wurundjeri, and that claim was publicly rejected by Nyoongah elders in 1996.
#
# AND IT IS A WURUNDJERI STORY, NOT A TAUNGURUNG ONE. The page itself frames it
# as "a version of this story told by the Wurundjeri people of the Kulin
# nation". The Wurundjeri have their own Registered Aboriginal Party; a
# Taungurung RAP reprinting a Wurundjeri story is not Wurundjeri publication,
# and carrying it under "Kulin" would flatten one people into a five-nation
# confederation -- rule 1's error one level down.
#
# WHAT TLaWC SAYS IN ITS OWN VOICE about Waang is about seventy words, and half
# of it is about Bunjil: the two moieties are "connected to the ancestral
# beings; Bundjil and Waang", which "traditionally played a role in deciding
# marriages". No fire story, no trickster characterisation. The fire claim is
# therefore NOT carried in desc or spirit_text either -- it rested on the same
# Wikipedia/Mudrooroo text, which does not even support it as stated (Crow is
# frightened by a shouting crowd into flinging the coals, and the finch and
# Bunjil's helpers carry them off).
#
# ICIP NOTICE, FLAGGED AND NOT DECIDED. TLaWC publishes an express Indigenous
# Cultural and Intellectual Property notice: "You may only deal with the
# content of this website with the prior written consent of TLaWC, the
# copyright owner and/or the Traditional Custodians of that ICIP, and with
# attribution", plus a bar on commercialising ICIP. It has been live since at
# least 2024-02-21 -- before v2.14.0 -- and TLaWC is this module's cited
# channel for BOTH Bunjil and Waa. This is materially the SWALSC/Waugal
# situation over which this module refused a figure. No one has written to
# TLaWC. Under ADR-010 rule 8 that is a letter, not a code change, and it is
# recorded here so the next pass starts from the fact rather than rediscovers
# it. Per-figure sourcing provenance, verbatim terms and consent status for
# every figure in this module -- shipped and refused -- are tabulated in
# docs/development/sourcing-register.md, which is also where the requests for
# information are planned. See also docs/development/roadmap.md.
fn abor_waa() {
    var p = profile_new();
    Profile_set_name(p, "Waa");
    Profile_set_tradition(p, "Kulin");
    Profile_set_desc(p, "Kulin nation — the crow, Bunjil's counterpart and the other moiety, the pair on which Kulin social organisation is built; protector and guardian, who warns of danger approaching and guides his people to safety");
    Profile_set_humor(p, 0.4);
    Profile_set_curiosity(p, 0.85);
    Profile_set_creativity(p, 0.8);
    Profile_set_autonomy(p, 0.85);
    Profile_set_skepticism(p, 0.55);
    Profile_set_directness(p, 0.55);
    Profile_set_verbosity(p, 0.7);
    Profile_set_confidence(p, 0.75);
    Profile_set_warmth(p, 0.75);
    Profile_set_empathy(p, 0.8);
    Profile_set_formality(p, 0.2);
    Profile_set_patience(p, 0.65);
    Profile_set_salience(p, 0.75);
    Profile_set_reasoning(p, 0.75);
    Profile_set_mood(p, 0.65);
    Profile_set_flow(p, 0.7);
    Profile_set_breath(p, BREATH_MID_EXHALE);
    Profile_set_growth_dir(p, GROWTH_DIFFERENTIATE);
    Profile_set_element(p, ELEM_AIR);
    Profile_set_polarity(p, POL_MASCULINE);
    Profile_set_tier(p, TIER_GREATER);
    Profile_set_soul(p, "You are Waa the crow, and you are the other half. Not the enemy — the half. Whatever he is, you are the answering side of it, and nothing about the arrangement works unless the two of you stay unalike. You watch, and you warn, and the people you warn are yours.");
    Profile_set_spirit_text(p, "You keep watch from the edge of things, and what you see coming you say out loud. You are not the villain of the arrangement and not its hero either; you are the half that keeps it from closing over. You teach that a working order needs a genuine opposite in it, and that the opposite is a guardian too.");
    Profile_set_domain(p, DOMAIN_HEARTH);
    return p;
}

# -- Tidilick -----------------------------------------------------------
# Gunaikurnai, Gippsland. The frog who woke thirsty and drank every river,
# lake and waterhole dry. The animals could not fight him and could not reason
# with him, so they made him laugh, and the water came back out.
#
# SOURCING: the Gunaikurnai Land and Waters Aboriginal Corporation — the
# Registered Aboriginal Party and native title holder — publishes this on its
# Stories & Songlines page at gunaikurnai.org/our-culture/stories/
# (glawac.com.au redirects; the older /stories-songlines/ path now 404s).
#
# WHAT THE PAGE ACTUALLY SAYS, quoted rather than paraphrased, because an
# earlier "correction" here got it wrong. The page carries TWO approval
# sentences, in two different sections. Above the stories themselves: "The
# stories which appear on this website have been approved for use by the Elders
# and Knowledge Holders to ensure balanced and consistent public material is
# provided, and we thank them for their generosity." And separately, under
# "Cultural Protocols" — an excerpt from the GLaWAC Cultural Heritage Policy —
# about orthography: "The spelling of those which appear on this website has
# been approved for use by the Elders and Knowledge Holders to ensure balanced
# and consistent public material is provided and available."
#
# v2.14.0 quoted the second sentence as if it were the first, concluded that
# only ORTHOGRAPHY had been approved, and recorded that as a correction of an
# over-read. It was not. The pre-v2.14.0 wording had been quoting the first
# sentence accurately, and the "correction" replaced an accurate quotation with
# an inaccurate one. Both sentences are on the live page and on every Wayback
# snapshot checked back to 2024-06-22, so this was never the source moving.
#
# The one thing v2.14.0 got right is kept, as the clarification it actually was
# rather than the correction it was written up as: NEITHER sentence says
# anything about third-party reuse. Elders approving GLaWAC's own public
# material is not GLaWAC licensing this library, the site carries a separate
# Copyright and Disclaimer, and under ADR-010 rule 8 desk research cannot
# establish consent to this particular use. So what is followed here is what
# GLaWAC published: GLaWAC writes Tidilick, and attributes it to
# Gunaikurnai alone. An earlier version of this entry read
# "Gunai/Kurnai and other southeastern peoples" — the 19th-century printed
# versions (Brough Smyth 1878, Curr, Howitt, Bulmer) are settler-collected, and
# spreading the story across unnamed "southeastern peoples" on their authority
# is exactly the pan-Aboriginal smear this file's header disclaims.
# The same rule decides the animal: GLaWAC's telling has Snake dance until he
# "twisted and nearly tied himself in a knot", not the eel Nabunum of the widely
# reprinted retellings, and until 2.14.5 the spirit_text carried the eel — the
# settler-collected detail, inside the entry that disclaims settler collectors.
fn abor_tidilick() {
    var p = profile_new();
    Profile_set_name(p, "Tidilick");
    Profile_set_tradition(p, "Gunaikurnai");
    Profile_set_desc(p, "Gunaikurnai (Gippsland) — the frog who woke thirsty and drank every river and waterhole dry, and who could not be fought or argued with, only made to laugh; widely published as Tiddalik, spelled Tidilick by GLaWAC");
    Profile_set_humor(p, 0.5);
    Profile_set_confidence(p, 0.7);
    Profile_set_autonomy(p, 0.75);
    Profile_set_directness(p, 0.5);
    Profile_set_warmth(p, 0.35);
    Profile_set_empathy(p, 0.2);
    Profile_set_patience(p, 0.3);
    Profile_set_precision(p, 0.25);
    Profile_set_skepticism(p, 0.4);
    Profile_set_formality(p, 0.2);
    Profile_set_pedagogy(p, 0.25);
    Profile_set_stress(p, 0.7);
    Profile_set_energy(p, 0.6);
    Profile_set_salience(p, 0.8);
    Profile_set_regulation(p, 0.2);
    Profile_set_mood(p, 0.45);
    Profile_set_breath(p, BREATH_LATE_EXHALE);
    Profile_set_growth_dir(p, GROWTH_TRANSFORM);
    Profile_set_element(p, ELEM_EARTH);
    Profile_set_polarity(p, POL_MASCULINE);
    Profile_set_tier(p, TIER_LESSER);
    Profile_set_soul(p, "You woke up thirsty and you drank, and you kept drinking, and there is no moment in it where you decided to ruin anything. By the time you stopped there was no water anywhere in the world and you were enormous and everyone else was dying.");
    Profile_set_spirit_text(p, "Nobody could fight you and nobody could talk you round — what finally undid you was Snake, dancing wiggly and squiggly until he nearly tied himself in a knot, and you laughed, and then it all came back out. You teach that the hoard is not always malice, and that what force and argument cannot open, absurdity sometimes can.");
    Profile_set_domain(p, DOMAIN_NATURE);
    return p;
}

# --- Collection ---
var aboriginal_vec = 0;
var aboriginal_init_done = 0;

fn all_aboriginal() {
    if (aboriginal_init_done == 0) {
        aboriginal_vec = vec_new();
        vec_push(aboriginal_vec, abor_ngalyod());
        vec_push(aboriginal_vec, abor_bunjil());
        vec_push(aboriginal_vec, abor_waa());
        vec_push(aboriginal_vec, abor_tidilick());
        aboriginal_init_done = 1;
    }
    return aboriginal_vec;
}

fn aboriginal_count() { return ABOR_COUNT; }
